Z||000016||theogre||01-22-2000||03:51 PM||||I would not recomend anyone install lights that don't meet DOT requirements.<P>55 watt "low beam only" likely means a modified driving light. Definatly not DOT legal. The fact he hasn't had problems in his reagon of Virginia means absolutly nothing. His cover that he won't sell doesn't look hard to replicate. If you look at the posted pic, it's flat or formed to a slight curve and held at 3 points by screws or rivits. You could get the right curve by heating a sheet of lexan and letting it lay over the existing headlight door untill cool. Then trim it to fit how you want it.<P>I wonder if that guy has talked to a lawyer yet. With the sue happy U.S. he could be in for a real suprise if someone is hurt in/by a car with those non legal lights. <P>Aside from making yourself a ticket magnet, not using properly aimed DOT legal lights can cause a wreck. Lights that are too bright can result in head on colisions and runnoffs because one or both driver is completely blinded. Baddly aimed or dim lights have other problems....<P>My concern about Toms lights would be the pattern they throw. being set into the hood would seem to me to limit the width of the pattern seriously. Effectivly giving you a good/bad case of night tunnel vision. Could throw some odd shadows. Tom, have you done a rough patern plot of the high and low beams yet? <BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||01-22-2000||04:10 PM||||Ditto to what ogre said!  Except for that last paragraph.  I hadn't thought about that.  Good point, though.   Food for thought (and anyone who has seen me knows I like food!).||||reg||1
Z||000018||Phil||01-22-2000||04:10 PM||||My concern is not so much the type of bulb but rather putting a clear cover on the light. I had a '67 Jag that had glass covers over the head lights, in '68 Jag removed them from their design because of new DOT requirements. Just wondering if this still holds true.||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||01-22-2000||04:26 PM||||From a technical stand point, the cover should not make much diferance to the light pattern. Where a cover becomes a problems is when it fogs up with moisture. That could really reduce light output.<P>There are currently a number of cars with factory covers on the lights, but most seem also to be those projector lamp setups. Not sealed beam or back loaded body contour sets.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Phil||01-22-2000||08:48 PM||||Aren't those light covers(on the new cars) more or less perpendicular to the bulb , refraction and all that? Also I believe they are optical quality material.||||reg||1
Z||000021||TOM||01-22-2000||09:04 PM||||All very good concerns. I have had mine installed for about two years now. I live in Los Angeles and have never had a cop look twice at me. Except that one that owned a fiero and wanted a set for his. Do you mean set back in the hood like all the new camaro's and covered like all the Z's since 89?||||reg||1
